WEBVTT

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:20.090
<b>LEGEND</b>

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:20.090
<b>OF THE</b>

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:20.090
<b>  GALACTIC HEROES</b>

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:44.640
One Hundred Billion Stars, One Hundred Billion Lights

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:44.640
Chapter 10

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:51.430
On September 11th of Space
Year 794, Imperial Year 485...

00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:54.570
...Reinhard and Kircheis
arrived at Iserlohn Fortress.

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:57.280
In preparation for the Free Planets
Alliance's large-scale attack...

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:00.930
...which was due to start in November or December of that year...

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:08.640
...they were a part of the gathering
Imperial Fleet sent to the Iserlohn area.

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:11.960
Did you hear that, Kircheis ?

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:16.160
The rebel forces are once again
proposing to Iserlohn Fortress.

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:19.500
How many times do they have to
be rejected, before they give up ?

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:22.280
It was not an easy
determination for them, I presume.

00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:25.940
Is there any
determination that's "easy" ?

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:27.800
I don't know of
any such thing.

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:31.440
I'm just joking.
Never mind.

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:33.680
I don't mind.

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:40.900
If I did, I would have had to
hang myself a long time ago.

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:45.730
When he realizes that he's behaving
like a spoiled child to Kircheis...

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:49.700
...Reinhard often acts like this
and abruptly changes the subject.

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:53.130
Iserlohn Fortress is the
source of all evils.

00:02:53.130 --> 00:02:59.580
Because the military leaders on both sides are so obsessed with it they needlessly restrict their options.

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:06.000
I guess it means their perspectives are
only as wide as Iserlohn's narrow corridor.

00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:08.510
You're right.

00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:12.530
Every year, there are numerous
skirmishes in this area...

00:03:12.530 --> 00:03:16.330
...and such unproductive battles
will continue on for eternity.

00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:21.070
To break up that chain of eternity,
there has to be a drastic change.

00:03:21.070 --> 00:03:25.130
And who else besides me is capable
of bringing about such a change ?

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:29.030
Do you already
have a plan in mind ?

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:32.960
My self-confidence
isn't without its basis.

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:38.540
If I were in charge I would create a system that does not require the presence of Iserlohn Fortress.

00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:44.000
But right now, I'm merely
in command of about 1000 ships.

00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:46.790
Lord Reinhard...

00:03:46.790 --> 00:03:49.810
I know, I shouldn't be hasty.

00:03:49.810 --> 00:03:53.980
Besides, this time around, we will
undoubtedly enter a large-scale fleet battle.

00:03:53.980 --> 00:04:01.370
Unlike the last time, it's not like Luneberg
will get in my way and take the initiative.

00:04:01.370 --> 00:04:05.750
Are you concerned about Rear
Admiral Luneberg, Lord Reinhard ?

00:04:05.750 --> 00:04:07.670
Huh ?

00:04:08.470 --> 00:04:09.800
A little bit.

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:13.580
Don't concern yourself
with Rear Admiral Luneberg.

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:18.840
If he gets in your way,
I'll do something about it.

00:04:19.780 --> 00:04:21.800
You only need
to look ahead.

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:24.630
You're right, Kircheis.

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:26.080
I'll count on you.

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:29.490
When I've left things up to you, there's never
been a case where things went wrong.

00:04:50.270 --> 00:04:51.350
Captain Kesler ?

00:05:01.930 --> 00:05:03.650
What brings you here ?

00:05:04.230 --> 00:05:09.920
I am here as an agent for the
Military Ministry High General Officer.

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:13.340
High General Officer, you mean...

00:05:13.340 --> 00:05:15.300
His Excellency,
Admiral Grimmelshausen.

00:05:16.570 --> 00:05:18.530
Right.
Of course.

00:05:18.530 --> 00:05:19.820
Is he well ?

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:26.960
At about the same time Rear Admiral
Musel left Odin, Admiral Grimmelshausen...

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:32.030
...fell ill with the flu and it got
worse and became pneumonia.

00:05:32.030 --> 00:05:33.750
Is it bad ?

00:05:33.750 --> 00:05:37.030
With the pneumonia, he's
gotten increasingly weaker...

00:05:37.030 --> 00:05:40.390
...and we wonder if he'll
last through the year.

00:05:40.390 --> 00:05:45.090
The Kaiser became worried and
sent people to look after him.

00:05:46.060 --> 00:05:47.420
It's that bad...

00:05:47.420 --> 00:05:50.040
Especially, because of his age.

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:53.390
And also, since he's been
removed from the frontline...

00:05:53.390 --> 00:05:57.080
...no, since he removed himself from
the frontline, he's not as energetic.

00:05:57.350 --> 00:06:00.020
I'm sorry to hear that.

00:06:01.610 --> 00:06:06.440
By the way, Captain Kesler, I troubled
you in a strange way on Odin.

00:06:06.440 --> 00:06:08.240
Oh, no, don't mention it.

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:09.630
Because of what you did...

00:06:09.630 --> 00:06:12.830
...I didn't have to embarrass Rear Admiral
Luneberg, whom I respect very much.

00:06:12.830 --> 00:06:14.300
I must thank you.

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:25.390
From what I heard, you're in command
of a whole unit of the fleet this time.

00:06:25.390 --> 00:06:29.990
In the previous campaign,
I was in command of about 100 ships.

00:06:29.990 --> 00:06:32.310
This time around, I'm
in charge of about 1,000.

00:06:32.310 --> 00:06:35.880
In the next campaign, I hope
to lead a fleet of 10,000.

00:06:38.710 --> 00:06:43.940
With your talent, I'm sure
it'll happen very soon.

00:06:45.010 --> 00:06:48.630
The problem is whether or not
people will acknowledge that "talent."

00:06:48.630 --> 00:06:54.200
By the way, is your visit here as an agent for the High General Officer meant to be public knowledge ?

00:06:56.860 --> 00:06:59.830
In our nation,
it's often difficult...

00:06:59.830 --> 00:07:03.680
...to distinguish between the orders of the
government and those of a high official.

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:06.130
I suppose this is one of the
smaller examples of such.

00:07:08.020 --> 00:07:11.940
As you say, there have been more
significant examples of such incidents.

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:16.800
In any case, I'm interested in finding
out what sorts of truths you know.

00:07:18.140 --> 00:07:20.920
We say, "Truth is
the daughter of Time."

00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:26.580
If you find out the truth too soon,
the child may not be born in good health...

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:30.320
...and the mother may
be endangered, too.

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:33.620
Do you honestly
believe that ?

00:07:33.910 --> 00:07:37.730
The ones who sent me
here seem to believe so.

00:07:37.730 --> 00:07:40.350
Just conjecture on my part.

00:07:40.350 --> 00:07:44.850
In any case, I don't think you
should get too involved in this.

00:07:57.980 --> 00:08:03.030
Meanwhile, the Free Planets Alliance
used their fleet of 36,900 ships...

00:08:03.030 --> 00:08:06.000
...to counter the Imperial Fleet
on their side of the Corridor...

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:11.630
...preventing the Empire from strategically
deploying outside the Iserlohn Corridor.

00:08:12.930 --> 00:08:17.790
The officer who proposed this decisive
measure at the General Staff Office...

00:08:17.790 --> 00:08:21.930
...was one of their fleet unit commanders,
Rear Admiral Willem Holland.

00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:27.250
In any case, we thought in the past that
the key to the battle at Iserlohn Fortress...

00:08:27.250 --> 00:08:34.040
...was how to prevent them from using the Thor Hammer, or how to make it useless.

00:08:34.040 --> 00:08:37.310
But I propose this plan.

00:08:38.350 --> 00:08:41.590
Rear Admiral Willem Holland was
almost 32 years old at the time...

00:08:41.590 --> 00:08:45.220
...a young blue-chip among the
generally youth-oriented Alliance force.

00:08:45.220 --> 00:08:47.450
First, we'll use our
main fleet as a decoy.

00:08:48.890 --> 00:08:54.330
Of course, we should avoid getting that
fleet decimated by the Thor Hammer.

00:08:54.330 --> 00:08:58.700
The range of the Thor Hammer
is known from previous battles.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:08.030
On the border of that range, at the
"Dead Line," which I'll name as "D-Line..."

00:09:08.030 --> 00:09:12.380
...we'll deploy our main fleet,
and continuously thrust and then retreat.

00:09:13.430 --> 00:09:18.950
Naturally, the opponent will send a large fleet to engage us and pull us inside of its range.

00:09:20.830 --> 00:09:26.810
In this way, we'll lure the enemy fleet toward the D-Line and while their line of defense gets thinner...

00:09:26.810 --> 00:09:32.830
...we'll send missile-cruisers through the
outermost periphery of the Corridor...

00:09:32.830 --> 00:09:38.300
...and then launch a massive missile
attack on the enemy fortress.

00:09:39.250 --> 00:09:44.600
Using the waterfall of firepower,
we'll destroy the walls of Iserlohn !

00:09:46.760 --> 00:09:50.830
The admirals weren't as passionate
about Holland's plan as he was.

00:09:50.830 --> 00:09:56.630
Some were even turned off by this proposal
from Holland, a front-line commander...

00:09:56.630 --> 00:10:00.530
...who was not a part of the General
Staff Office, but everyone kept quiet...

00:10:00.530 --> 00:10:02.750
...because no one had any
meaningful alternative plan.

00:10:04.020 --> 00:10:08.130
But this proposal
was accepted anyway.

00:10:08.130 --> 00:10:09.620
Why is that ?

00:10:09.620 --> 00:10:11.830
There's someone in the Staff
Office at General Headquarters...

00:10:11.830 --> 00:10:15.030
...a Commander
named Andrew Fork...

00:10:15.030 --> 00:10:20.130
I know, he was the valedictorian of the
year after us at the military academy.

00:10:20.580 --> 00:10:24.100
He was a gloomy one, but I see,
he's already become a Commander.

00:10:25.140 --> 00:10:27.860
If you meet him, at least
salute him, Lieutenant Commander.

00:10:27.860 --> 00:10:31.310
And that Commander Fork guy
brought a similar proposal.

00:10:31.310 --> 00:10:34.630
Fleet Admiral Lobos thinks very
highly of him, so it turned out...

00:10:34.630 --> 00:10:37.380
"If he is the proponent of
that plan, then we'll take it."

00:10:38.890 --> 00:10:43.710
They decided on the proposal not for the
proposal, but for who submitted the proposal ?

00:10:43.710 --> 00:10:45.630
As always, what a bunch of...

00:10:45.630 --> 00:10:50.210
So there's the lesson for you:
always watch how you behave.

00:10:50.210 --> 00:10:54.300
In any case, can a destroyer commander be idly hanging out in a place like this ?

00:10:54.930 --> 00:10:59.460
On my ship, the staff is so industrious that a name only commander has a lot of time on his hands.

00:10:59.460 --> 00:11:04.320
So I figured I'd just come to see my elder who
also seems to have so much time on his hands.

00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:07.350
I have too much to
do and so little time.

00:11:07.350 --> 00:11:10.480
Because Vice Admiral
Cerebrese has disappeared...

00:11:10.480 --> 00:11:13.460
...all the supply-related work
has been transferred over to me.

00:11:13.460 --> 00:11:15.690
Yes, I understand very well.

00:11:15.690 --> 00:11:19.650
It's all because of you, Commodore Cazellnu, that we're able to eat three meals every day...

00:11:19.650 --> 00:11:22.930
...and I bow to you three times each day unbeknown to anyone and everyone.

00:11:22.930 --> 00:11:25.300
Stop that, it's gross.

00:11:25.300 --> 00:11:27.630
What I mean by the "superior who
has too much time on his hands"...

00:11:27.630 --> 00:11:31.020
...is that part-time
staff officer over there.

00:11:31.020 --> 00:11:32.230
Huh ?

00:11:33.200 --> 00:11:37.180
What do you think, Yang,
about Operation Waltz on D-Line ?

00:11:37.180 --> 00:11:40.610
That name isn't in particularly
good taste, I think...

00:11:40.610 --> 00:11:43.880
I'm not talking about the name -
what do you think about the content ?

00:11:46.200 --> 00:11:49.170
I don't think
it's that bad.

00:11:49.170 --> 00:11:52.050
So you mean you're
passively supportive ?

00:11:52.050 --> 00:11:55.820
Well, the best plan is
not to attack Iserlohn.

00:11:56.320 --> 00:11:57.440
That's a great idea.

00:11:57.440 --> 00:11:59.850
That way, we won't have
more unnecessary casualties.

00:12:01.280 --> 00:12:07.890
No, I mean, we're in a war against the
Galactic Empire and not Iserlohn Fortress.

00:12:09.050 --> 00:12:14.060
When that fortress was built, everyone
around us fell into the false impression...

00:12:14.060 --> 00:12:18.390
...that unless we get past Iserlohn
Fortress, we won't win the war.

00:12:21.180 --> 00:12:26.480
In any case, I don't think it's possible to get
Iserlohn Fortress to fall through sheer force.

00:12:26.480 --> 00:12:27.490
Yeah.

00:12:29.320 --> 00:12:31.800
Well, I think I'll go now.

00:12:32.170 --> 00:12:34.530
Don't be too playful
and lose your ship.

00:12:34.530 --> 00:12:37.280
Your jokes won't have
much effect on the enemy.

00:12:37.280 --> 00:12:38.670
Yeah, yeah, I know.

00:12:47.080 --> 00:12:54.000
By the way, Admiral Greenhill's daughter graduated from military school with the second best grades.

00:12:54.890 --> 00:12:56.250
She must be
a talented woman.

00:12:56.250 --> 00:12:57.800
And she's beautiful.

00:12:59.930 --> 00:13:01.190
You're not interested, are you ?

00:13:01.760 --> 00:13:05.890
I wouldn't say I have no interest, but quite
frankly, she's someone from outside my world.

00:13:06.500 --> 00:13:11.760
Plus, she's the daughter of your superior,
so it's more annoying than anything else, eh ?

00:13:11.760 --> 00:13:13.710
Well, that's so like you.

00:13:17.360 --> 00:13:19.290
Oh yeah, how's Julian doing ?

00:13:19.290 --> 00:13:20.170
He's doing very well.

00:13:20.170 --> 00:13:21.610
He's gotten a little taller, too.

00:13:22.420 --> 00:13:26.630
Because the mission is to
attack Iserlohn Fortress...

00:13:26.630 --> 00:13:30.290
...it's only natural to deploy
a large-scale ground force.

00:13:30.290 --> 00:13:35.130
And it's inevitable that the Rosenritter
are among the ground forces for this battle.

00:13:35.710 --> 00:13:39.920
Walter von Schenkopp was promoted
to Colonel just before this mission...

00:13:39.920 --> 00:13:44.840
...and was also officially appointed to be the
13th commander of the Rosenritter Regiment.

00:13:45.440 --> 00:13:47.630
After the loss
at Vanfleet 4-2...

00:13:47.630 --> 00:13:52.080
...he was held responsible and
was kept as interim commander...

00:13:52.080 --> 00:13:54.300
...but he finally
got the promotion.

00:13:55.500 --> 00:14:01.630
But it's probably a promotion in advance
and also a message to return with results...

00:14:01.630 --> 00:14:03.800
...even if it costs your life.

00:14:03.800 --> 00:14:08.240
Maybe it's a message that we
should all return in caskets.

00:14:09.140 --> 00:14:13.530
Linz and Blumhart were both promoted
to Major and Captain, respectively...

00:14:13.530 --> 00:14:16.180
...but they were apparently in
no mood to celebrate, either.

00:14:18.510 --> 00:14:22.000
During this campaign, we'll put an
end to our battle with Luneberg.

00:14:24.140 --> 00:14:31.650
The Rosenritter can only get a new
start after killing that former commander.

00:14:33.140 --> 00:14:38.200
But how do we find him
on this large battlefield ?

00:14:38.200 --> 00:14:40.060
That's the difficult issue.

00:14:41.300 --> 00:14:51.490
Every chance we get, we'll announce to the Galactic Empire that the Rosenritter are alive and well.

00:14:51.490 --> 00:14:54.490
With capital
letters in Gothic font.

00:14:54.490 --> 00:15:00.030
If we do that, Luneberg will have to
enter the battlefield himself to defeat us.

00:15:01.270 --> 00:15:05.980
It's the plight of the defector,
but it's a path he chose himself.

00:15:05.980 --> 00:15:10.930
Otherwise, he won't be able to defend his position or his honor, so I'm sure that's what will happen.

00:15:10.930 --> 00:15:12.940
And if he still
doesn't show up ?

00:15:12.940 --> 00:15:17.090
Then I'll just tell them that Luneberg
is actually a secret agent of the Alliance...

00:15:17.090 --> 00:15:20.250
...who's pretending
to be a defector.

00:15:20.250 --> 00:15:24.700
It's not like they're retaining
Luneberg for his personal virtues.

00:15:25.720 --> 00:15:27.960
Colonel, you're
such a schemer.

00:15:27.960 --> 00:15:31.730
In that case, I suppose
Luneberg will have no choice.

00:15:31.730 --> 00:15:34.070
This is just Strategy 101.

00:15:34.070 --> 00:15:37.080
And for someone like me, the
basics are the best I can do...

00:15:37.080 --> 00:15:40.500
...and I can't expect more
improvement out of myself.

00:15:41.390 --> 00:15:44.210
And Schenkopp had
a personal reason, too.

00:15:44.210 --> 00:15:47.730
Only by killing the talented and
fearless former commander...

00:15:47.730 --> 00:15:52.190
...Luneberg, who is responsible for
his lover's and subordinate's deaths...

00:15:52.190 --> 00:15:56.450
...can he psychologically allow
himself to have a new start.

00:15:59.330 --> 00:16:06.930
Between October and November of 
Space Year 794, Empire Year 485...

00:16:06.930 --> 00:16:11.530
...near the Alliance side
of the Iserlohn Corridor...

00:16:11.530 --> 00:16:15.280
...small-scale battles occurred
for control of its space.

00:16:17.690 --> 00:16:20.830
The battles involved anywhere
from 50 to 3000 ships...

00:16:20.830 --> 00:16:25.530
...in contention over each of the several
thousand cubic A.U. spaces of that area.

00:16:26.470 --> 00:16:30.810
Reinhard also led his fleet and had contributed
to more than 20 skirmishes already...

00:16:30.810 --> 00:16:35.180
...and as if to indulge himself in the act of
hunting, he demonstrated his naval skills.

00:16:36.120 --> 00:16:38.030
But a unit of
about 1000 ships...

00:16:38.030 --> 00:16:41.010
...did not have enough influence on the
larger outcome of the conflict as a whole...

00:16:41.010 --> 00:16:44.210
...and Imperial Naval Headquarters did not
particularly note his accomplishments.

00:16:45.210 --> 00:16:48.030
Rather, his accomplishments may have
instead opened the Alliance's eyes...

00:16:48.030 --> 00:16:51.870
...to his presence as a
little-known but shrewd tactician.

00:16:53.600 --> 00:16:59.530
If we had been flanked there,
all of us could have been annihilated.

00:16:59.530 --> 00:17:01.610
That must have
been a close call.

00:17:01.610 --> 00:17:05.280
I guess it's a job well done,
for an aristocratic profligate...

00:17:05.280 --> 00:17:07.840
...whose only obsessions are alcohol
and women at Neue Sanssouci.

00:17:09.450 --> 00:17:13.180
If they knew exactly
whom they were facing...

00:17:13.180 --> 00:17:16.050
...they probably wouldn't have been
so cavalier about the situation.

00:17:17.080 --> 00:17:20.730
On November 6th, an alliance fleet
led by Rear Admiral Ramsay Wartz...

00:17:20.730 --> 00:17:24.500
...consisting of 2,500 ships,
encountered Reinhard's fleet.

00:17:25.620 --> 00:17:28.080
We discovered the enemy fleet;
the number is roughly 1,000 !

00:17:30.250 --> 00:17:32.210
The opposition fleet
is densely conglomerated.

00:17:32.210 --> 00:17:36.850
We should spread the wings,
surround and attack them.

00:17:37.280 --> 00:17:39.720
Spread both wings and
surround the enemy fleet !

00:17:41.260 --> 00:17:46.030
The adviser of the Wartz Fleet, Marcom
Wideborn was 27 years old at the time.

00:17:46.030 --> 00:17:50.300
At the military school, he was said to be
a once-every-ten-year kind of genius.

00:17:50.300 --> 00:17:51.880
The enemy is...

00:17:51.880 --> 00:17:53.230
What is it ?

00:17:53.230 --> 00:17:55.530
The enemy fleet is
rapidly closing in on us !

00:17:55.530 --> 00:17:56.230
What ?

00:17:56.840 --> 00:17:59.880
Reinhard chose to concentrate his
forces and planned to focus his attack...

00:17:59.880 --> 00:18:03.020
...on the core of the enemy fleet
to make it collapse from there.

00:18:07.350 --> 00:18:08.420
This is outrageous.

00:18:08.420 --> 00:18:09.310
This can't be happening.

00:18:09.310 --> 00:18:10.560
It defies commonsense...

00:18:13.840 --> 00:18:18.650
Having lost their flagship, the Wartz Fleet
collapsed under Reinhard's attack...

00:18:18.650 --> 00:18:23.790
...and only 300 ships narrowly
escaped from the fray.

00:18:26.990 --> 00:18:31.770
Later on November 14th, the fleet of fast
cruisers under Rear Admiral Cabot...

00:18:31.770 --> 00:18:34.790
...was massacred by an
unexpected rear attack.

00:18:36.170 --> 00:18:39.230
When occurrences like this happen again
and again in such a short period of time...

00:18:39.230 --> 00:18:42.030
...the Alliance Fleet couldn't
help but be cognizant of them.

00:18:42.030 --> 00:18:45.500
The Imperial Fleet has
quite a smartass commander.

00:18:45.500 --> 00:18:50.600
Our losses in recent days are
mostly at his hands, aren't they ?

00:18:51.320 --> 00:18:52.560
I would think so.

00:18:53.230 --> 00:18:54.640
What do you plan to do ?

00:18:56.160 --> 00:18:57.880
Chief General Officer Greenhill...

00:18:57.880 --> 00:19:01.400
...wanted to concentrate on planning
the attack on Iserlohn Fortress itself...

00:19:01.400 --> 00:19:05.020
...so he decided to leave the plans against
this "smartass" commander to someone else.

00:19:05.830 --> 00:19:10.230
Admiral Greenhill ordered Yang - who was
known as "good-for-nothing Yang"...

00:19:10.230 --> 00:19:13.710
...to take care of the enemy,
and gave him the necessary files.

00:19:13.710 --> 00:19:15.330
He remembered that
six years earlier...

00:19:15.330 --> 00:19:18.880
...Yang had rescued a large number
of non-combatants from El Facil...

00:19:18.880 --> 00:19:22.190
...and was hailed as a hero.

00:19:23.540 --> 00:19:26.870
Other officers knew it too, but they tended
to dismiss that occurrence as a fluke...

00:19:26.870 --> 00:19:31.150
...as they witnessed
Yang's behavior firsthand.

00:19:31.940 --> 00:19:36.050
One day later, Yang submitted
a battle plan to Greenhill.

00:19:37.120 --> 00:19:38.160
Okay.

00:19:38.160 --> 00:19:39.640
I'll accept the plan.

00:19:40.430 --> 00:19:41.770
Right.

00:19:41.770 --> 00:19:43.990
I have one favor to ask.

00:19:43.990 --> 00:19:45.660
Go ahead.

00:19:45.660 --> 00:19:50.400
Can we pretend that you
proposed this battle plan ?

00:19:51.440 --> 00:19:56.440
But that means I can't acknowledge
your contribution to this proposal.

00:19:56.440 --> 00:19:58.740
That's not the right way
to do things in the military.

00:19:59.610 --> 00:20:05.710
Well, if the fleet finds out it's my plan,
they won't execute it as seriously.

00:20:06.460 --> 00:20:10.540
It's better to say that it's the plan
proposed by the Chief General Officer.

00:20:12.580 --> 00:20:18.580
Oh, and if you'll excuse me, I'd like to ask that
you won't be stingy with the number of ships...

00:20:18.580 --> 00:20:21.580
...as our chances may
elude us in that case.

00:20:21.580 --> 00:20:24.680
In accordance with this
plan, on November 19th...

00:20:24.680 --> 00:20:28.580
...Reinhard was almost captured
by the enemy fleet's enclosure.

00:20:29.850 --> 00:20:33.860
That day, Reinhard was testing a tactic
that was very difficult to execute...

00:20:33.860 --> 00:20:37.110
...to retreat in front of the enemy
fleet and fire while withdrawing...

00:20:37.110 --> 00:20:40.590
...and as the enemy fleet
chases and protrudes...

00:20:40.590 --> 00:20:45.620
...to pass through their side to their
rear and attack them from behind.

00:20:47.120 --> 00:20:49.480
In fact, he had been testing
such fleet maneuvers...

00:20:49.480 --> 00:20:54.840
...and battle deployment theories
in his previous engagements.

00:20:55.630 --> 00:20:57.360
Did you see that, Kircheis ?

00:20:57.360 --> 00:21:00.270
That ship destroyed
two enemy ships on its own.

00:21:00.940 --> 00:21:01.780
Yes.

00:21:01.780 --> 00:21:05.050
It was a very
skillful battle execution.

00:21:05.050 --> 00:21:07.040
Who is in command
of that ship ?

00:21:07.040 --> 00:21:12.820
The commander is
Captain Fritz Joseph Bittenfeld.

00:21:12.820 --> 00:21:17.520
It looks reckless but he really
leads timely and successful attacks.

00:21:17.520 --> 00:21:19.860
When things settle down,
I'd like to see him.

00:21:19.860 --> 00:21:20.950
How old is he ?

00:21:20.950 --> 00:21:23.930
According to the record,
he's 27 years old.

00:21:23.930 --> 00:21:25.770
Oh, he's fairly young.

00:21:26.880 --> 00:21:27.780
What ?

00:21:29.330 --> 00:21:33.730
It happened when Reinhard just finished
deploying behind the enemy fleet.

00:21:33.730 --> 00:21:37.160
From the top, bottom, and back, the enemy
reinforcements came swarming toward him.

00:21:38.680 --> 00:21:44.130
They couldn't have known each other's names at this point but Yang was cognizant of Reinhard's plan.

00:21:44.130 --> 00:21:46.480
Yang had discovered that
the "smartass" enemy...

00:21:46.480 --> 00:21:51.480
...was testing many different deployment
tactics and foresaw that he would try the...

00:21:51.480 --> 00:21:54.540
..."withdraw and pass through the side
to attack from behind" maneuver.

00:21:54.540 --> 00:21:57.880
Moreover, Yang had analyzed the
positioning of Reinhard's fleet...

00:21:57.880 --> 00:22:01.880
...and reserved a fleet of
10,000 ships for enclosure...

00:22:01.880 --> 00:22:04.940
...before leaving the battle
execution to Admiral Greenhill.

00:22:05.900 --> 00:22:10.090
But in actuality, the Alliance was as stingy
with the ship deployment as Yang feared...

00:22:10.090 --> 00:22:12.560
...and couldn't form
a complete enclosure.

00:22:13.850 --> 00:22:19.480
Reinhard attacked the weakest link and
successfully escaped from the encirclement...

00:22:19.480 --> 00:22:21.700
...but lost about 1/3 of
his fleet in the process.

00:22:23.030 --> 00:22:27.030
Our losses up until this point
were only 30 ships, but...

00:22:27.030 --> 00:22:28.330
Lord Reinhard...

00:22:29.030 --> 00:22:30.760
I know.

00:22:30.760 --> 00:22:34.130
The enemy won't
just go down quietly.

00:22:34.130 --> 00:22:36.130
I guess I was a
little overconfident.

00:22:39.080 --> 00:22:41.280
It seems the enemy
has a smart guy, too.

00:22:46.430 --> 00:22:48.080
I guess they got away.

00:22:48.080 --> 00:22:49.650
Regrettably so.

00:22:49.650 --> 00:22:50.910
But that's all right.

00:22:50.910 --> 00:22:55.730
It's enough to break the
nose of that smartass enemy.

00:22:55.730 --> 00:23:01.060
Our primary objective is to attack Iserlohn Fortress itself so we can't be bothered with issues like this.

00:23:08.520 --> 00:23:10.940
I guess that's about
as good as it gets.

00:23:10.940 --> 00:23:14.430
At least we made the
Imperial Fleet retreat...

00:23:14.430 --> 00:23:17.880
...but their basic strategy is to lure us
inside the area of Iserlohn Fortress...

00:23:17.880 --> 00:23:22.300
...so this retreat may also just
be in accordance with their plan.

00:23:22.670 --> 00:23:26.240
The battle was moving toward the
space area of Iserlohn Fortress.

00:23:26.240 --> 00:23:30.430
If you looked at that fact alone, it seemed
that the Alliance was winning the battle...

00:23:30.430 --> 00:23:34.310
...but as Yang thought, the retreat was
in accordance with the Empire's plan.

00:23:36.040 --> 00:23:40.330
Reinhard's fleet also returned to
Iserlohn Fortress for the time being...

00:23:40.330 --> 00:23:44.350
...for repairs and resupply in preparation
for the decisive battles to follow.

00:23:47.350 --> 00:23:48.880
On December 1st...

00:23:48.880 --> 00:23:54.530
...the Free Planets Alliance finally deployed
all of their fleets in front of Iserlohn Fortress.

00:23:56.170 --> 00:23:59.480
The enemy fleet is presently out
of the the Thor Hammer's range...

00:23:59.480 --> 00:24:02.260
...holding 6.4 light seconds away.

00:24:03.260 --> 00:24:05.170
The number of ships,
approximately 30,000 !

00:24:06.180 --> 00:24:08.330
Deploy our fleets.

00:24:09.430 --> 00:24:12.090
Drag the enemy into the
Thor Hammer's range.

00:24:31.980 --> 00:24:34.150
The Sixth Battle of
Iserlohn Fortress commences

00:24:37.830 --> 00:24:38.950
Open fire !

00:24:41.350 --> 00:24:44.580
The party of massacre and
destruction finally raised its curtain.

00:24:41.730 --> 00:24:46.570
Truth is the Daughter of Time

